--gray\"></div> The bottom line is: Don't interpret Wisdom as some good/evil or nice/mean spectrum. It's not. It's an alien code of behavior for beings who have transcended humanity. </div> </div> </div>I didn't. Don't put words in my mouth.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/904596-geniuses-and-gramarie\" target=\"_blank\">Genius templates</a>: Super Science Mini-Template for Demon: the Descent<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1337874-oracle-the-endbringers-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Oracle the Endbringers</a>: Time-Manipulator Fan-Splat","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","name":"milo v3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8331_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1505"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-16T02:56:49-07:00","id":1403075,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>galivet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1402680-lex-magica-and-time-travel?p=1403042#post1403042\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Supernal Magic is broader than spellcasting.<br /> <br /> Mana, soul stones, supernal beings, are all supernal phenomenon. They're all magical. Degeneration in those cases falls neatly under my thesis that acts of hubris always involve the mage's interactions with spells and other supernal phenomenon.<br /> <br /> Both of your items keying off the word &quot;action&quot; are cases I had in mind where the authors don't want to write &quot;with magic&quot; a hundred times in that section. Relevant actions in that context would be the activation of an imbued item, the summoning of a supernal being, flaring a nimbus, etc... and this covers the remainder of your points too. Those aren't referring to mundane actions, unless of course the ST wants to make use of the flex built into the Wisdom system to make it so and blunt the horror build into the mage template.<br /> <br /> You didn't address how your interpretation of &quot;mystic self&quot; for legacy attainments makes any sense in light of my point above about there being no exceptions for usages of legacy attainments that are &quot;immoral&quot; (according to a Western concept of morality? Eastern? Atlantean?) and also run utterly counter to the fundaments of the legacy. In no sense would a mage's &quot;mystic self&quot; be attuned to such actions... yet they're degeneration free anyway. It's just a nonsense position. You may as well say that they're free of risk of degeneration <i>because reasons</i>. A legacy is not defined by its attainments; the attainments arise out of and in support of the ethos of the legacy.<br /> <br /> Thematically, Wisdom and the Lex Magica being divorced from the common man's perception of morality is a fundamental driver of horror in Mage. A mage of Enlightened Wisdom could be a stone-cold murderer as long he's very careful, calculating, and judicious in how he employs magic, and the letter of the Lex Magica won't necessarily find him culpable for anything so long as his actions don't step on any other mage's toes. Once you've signed your name to a Watchtower, your God is magic and your morality is all about showing it proper respect.<br /> <br /> EDIT: To clarify, this is the meaning of &quot;mystic self is completely attuned to their use&quot;:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">&quot;The mage invokes them from her reshaped soul <b>instead of reaching into the Supernal</b>.&quot;</div>It means that they're effects that arise from the mage's own soul and are not supernal magic. And so, of course, their use has no impact on Wisdom, because Wisdom is only concerned with the mage's actions related to Supernal magic and other Supernal phenomenon. This is the interpretation of this collection of rules that is internally consistent. The other interpretation that you're advocating strikes me as &quot;It doesn't risk degeneration to give it an attractive gameplay advantage, even though that stands in contradiction to the setting.&quot; Going by your interpretation, the game should only have allowed legacy attainments to be degeneration free when the use aligns with the ethos of the legacy.<br /> <br /> EDIT 2: Honestly, I just don't get why someone would interpret Wisdom as reflecting even in part a common notion of morality/consience, as if a comprehensible character would ever think &quot;Mudering babies is deeply wrong... but I'm cool with it as long as I use this inured spell or legacy attainment. Then my sense of morality isn't violated so no risk of degeneration!&quot; That's just... random. What does make sense though is a character thinking &quot;My provincial notions of morality have burned away in the fires of Supernal Truth, but I must employ these Truths judiciously lest they consume me completely.&quot; In that paradigm, the mage's Wisdom isn't bothered by baby-murdering regardless; the only true sin is the reckless use of magic. That's consistent and comprehensible. A guy doesn't become a Rapt because he got drunk and shot his wife. He becomes a Rapt by using magic so carelessly that he loses all ability to take care in its use. Morals are for mortals.<br /> <br /> Now, this isn't to say that a mage wouldn't feel moral qualms about baby-murdering. We still have to have sympathetic, relatable characters. It's just that these violations of conscience and moral qualms aren't relevant for the Wisdom stat. The bottom line is that Mage does not have a stat that tracks a Good vs. Evil scale, it has a stat that tracks the Judicious vs Reckless scale with respect to the Supernal.<br /> <br /> EDIT 3: But at this point what I really want is for Satchel to prove me wrong by referencing a part of a 2E Mage fiction where a character struggles with Wisdom after a hit-and-run accident, or a DaveB 2E liveplay where he makes someone roll for degeneration after robbing someone at gunpoint, or similar.</div> </div> </div> </div>1.) No one has said Wisdom is a measure of morality. It's a measure of control. No need to argue a point no one is making.<br /> <br /> 2.)A few additional quotes:<br /> <br /> &quot;These rules only apply to people who would incur a breaking <br /> point or act of hubris for committing (or attempting) \u201cmurder.\u201d <br /> Creatures that don\u2019t have a problem killing people in general <br /> can ignore surrender without penalty and don\u2019t have the fight <br /> beaten out of them like normal folks.&quot;<br /> If mages wouldn't suffer AoH for murder under regular circumstances, they'd be able to ignore surrender.<br /> <br /> &quot;Using a human shield is almost certainly a breaking point or Act <br /> of Hubris.&quot;<br /> That is a purely mundane and physical action.<br /> <br /> Going through with a <br /> killing blow is an Act of Hubris for mages with Enlightened and <br /> Understanding Wisdom, whether the victim survives or not.&quot;<br /> Another physical and mundane action.<br /> <br /> Being soulless makes any instance of engaging in your Vice an AoH, though I admit it's pretty weird circumstances.<br /> <br /> &quot;Chopping up a Sarira is a Low Act of Hubris.&quot;<br /> From SoS. While the Sarira is magical, the act of chopping it up is not.<br /> <br /> &quot;The difference <br /> is that a Malefactor\u2019s Fault is a non-magical crime committed in <br /> pursuit of his arcane addictions, though he <i>might</i> use magic to <br /> do it.&quot;<br /> From N&amp;A.<br /> <br /> &quot;Savant behavior doesn\u2019t seem that<br /> strange by Awakened standards; by comparison, most mages are <br /> socialized throughout their lives to avoid <i>the mundane acts that <br /> destroy Wisdom</i>, and to condemn them in others.&quot;<br /> Also from N&amp;A.<br /> <br /> &quot;Enraptured mages who <i>lost their Wisdom to a mundane <br /> Act of Hubris</i> \u2014 usually Malefactors \u2014 gain a Fault based on <br /> using magic to repeat that act indefinitely,&quot;<br /> Again from N&amp;A.<br /> <br /> And I want to repeat: Nowhere does it actually say Wisdom only measure a mage's use of magic and that Acts of Hubris are only suffered through magical acts. It measures how much control they have over their own actions, with the introductory lines referring to how Wisdom ties back into their magic. Proving that you have poor self control through mundane means is reflected in having poor self control with your magic.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":11887,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","user":"Tessie","date":". 07-16-2020, 05:59 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-16T07:03:06-07:00","id":1403092,"title":"","content":"Conceded, although for the purpose of my own games I prefer the way I've been running it because my interpretation supports the &quot;Mages are monsters too&quot; horror aspect of the setting. I find that in games where characters lose a stat value based on the morality of their actions that they must spend XP to buy back or risk losing their character, it over-incentivizes them to act like goody-two-shoes superheroes and inhibits their willingness to really inhabit their characters. I mean, explain how &quot;Using a human shield&quot; is an Act of Hubris on anything other than moral grounds.<br /> <br /> And, as noted previously at length, I'm not able to jive the mechanical benefits of inured spells and legacy attainments w.r.t. degeneration into that schema. The fact that a mage used an inured spell or legacy attainment to kill someone in a fit of rage has no bearing on their self control. The intuitive notion of self control is about what you do, not about what tool you use to do it. Inured spells and legacy attainments being free of risk of degeneration end up seeming like gameplay advantages or consessions that run counter to the spirit of the Wisdom mechanic rather than supporting it, whatever hand-wavy throw-away justification is provided. It's like having a law against dumping raw sewage in a river, and finding out it's okay as long as the raw sewage is transported in barrels labeled &quot;soil&quot; because, hey, that law has harsh penalties and you have to cut the waste management companies a few breaks.<br /> <br /> If Wisdom is about self-control, it would have made more sense for a mage to be able to inure himself to specific misdeeds, and for a member of a legacy not risk degeneration for actions in line with the ethos of the legacy.<br /> <br /> Ugh, it just seems like a mess. Dish-served-cold murder <i>is</i> an act of self-control. Taking a human shield is a neutral action with respect to self-control. The waters are muddy, and it just looks like &quot;actions that a reasonable person would call selfish or evil are low Wisdom&quot; -- i.e. a measure of morality. This is starting to seem like an Emperor with No Clothes situation. It's like a hodgepodge, where when the authors wanted to throw in a mechanical penalty for an action they wanted to discourage they labeled it as an Act of Hubris for want of any other suitable mechanic. If there is a theme that underpins and unifies all these Acts of Hubris, it's not the one described in the section on Wisdom.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13121,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13121-galivet","user":"galivet","date":". 07-16-2020, 10:34 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13121-galivet","name":"galivet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13121_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"283"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-16T08:48:30-07:00","id":1403103,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>galivet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1402680-lex-magica-and-time-travel?p=1403092#post1403092\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Conceded, although for the purpose of my own games I prefer the way I've been running it because my interpretation supports the &amp;quot;Mages are monsters too&amp;quot; horror aspect of the setting. I find that in games where characters lose a stat value based on the morality of their actions that they must spend XP to buy back or risk losing their character, it over-incentivizes them to act like goody-two-shoes superheroes and inhibits their willingness to really inhabit their characters. I mean, explain how &amp;quot;Using a human shield&amp;quot; is an Act of Hubris on anything other than moral grounds.<br /> <br /> And, as noted previously at length, I'm not able to jive the mechanical benefits of inured spells and legacy attainments w.r.t. degeneration into that schema. The fact that a mage used an inured spell or legacy attainment to kill someone in a fit of rage has no bearing on their self control. The intuitive notion of self control is about what you do, not about what tool you use to do it. Inured spells and legacy attainments being free of risk of degeneration end up seeming like gameplay advantages or consessions that run counter to the spirit of the Wisdom mechanic rather than supporting it, whatever hand-wavy throw-away justification is provided. It's like having a law against dumping raw sewage in a river, and finding out it's okay as long as the raw sewage is transported in barrels labeled &amp;quot;soil&amp;quot; because, hey, that law has harsh penalties and you have to cut the waste management companies a few breaks.<br /> <br /> If Wisdom is about self-control, it would have made more sense for a mage to be able to inure himself to specific misdeeds, and for a member of a legacy not risk degeneration for actions in line with the ethos of the legacy.<br /> <br /> Ugh, it just seems like a mess. Dish-served-cold murder <i>is</i> an act of self-control. Taking a human shield is a neutral action with respect to self-control. The waters are muddy, and it just looks like &amp;quot;actions that a reasonable person would call selfish or evil are low Wisdom&amp;quot; -- i.e. a measure of morality. This is starting to seem like an Emperor with No Clothes situation. It's like a hodgepodge, where when the authors wanted to throw in a mechanical penalty for an action they wanted to discourage they labeled it as an Act of Hubris for want of any other suitable mechanic. If there is a theme that underpins and unifies all these Acts of Hubris, it's not the one described in the section on Wisdom.</div> </div> </div> </div>Time ago i gave the same interpretation to act of hubris.<br /> Reading nameless and accursed i realized that there was contradictions to this interpretation, but i decided to keep it as a house rule for reason similar to yours","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24346-neos01","name":"Neos01","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24346_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1021"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-16T09:38:17-07:00","id":1403107,"title":"","content":"I've always interpreted Wisdom as Self Control AND the ability to understand the value of life/good judgement. That's why mundane stuff like the human shield is there. The whole point of losing Wisdom is a Mage becoming less in touch with humanity and perceptions","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24173-primordial-newcomer","name":"Primordial newcomer","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1965"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-16T19:46:12-07:00","id":1403172,"title":"","content":"Morality makes sense only or people that cannot rewind time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2535-arthexis","name":"arthexis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2535_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"577"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-16T23:04:36-07:00","id":1403181,"title":"","content":".... killing people and using people as human shields is an act of hubris for the same sort of reasoning forcing people to do actions against their will is. It's depriving others of their agency and believing you have the right to decide who lives and dies. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\">Morality makes sense only or people that cannot rewind time.</span> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"font-family:inherit\"><br /> That's a pretty messed up viewpoint to me. That's sorta like considering any action morally okay in the real world as long as people don't catch you.</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/904596-geniuses-and-gramarie\" target=\"_blank\">Genius templates</a>: Super Science Mini-Template for Demon: the Descent<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1337874-oracle-the-endbringers-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Oracle the Endbringers</a>: Time-Manipulator Fan-Splat","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","name":"milo v3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8331_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1505"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-17T14:01:31-07:00","id":1403282,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>milo v3</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1402680-lex-magica-and-time-travel?p=1403181#post1403181\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's a pretty messed up viewpoint to me. That's sorta like considering any action morally okay in the real world as long as people don't catch you.[/FONT]</div> </div> </div> </div>lack of victims and lack of witnesses is a tangible difference, if subtle in the form of this thread's topic. So I don't think that's a valid comparison.<br /> <br /> A better one is the guy that pays people off to do fucked up stuff and explore their limits.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse/visitormessage/1408635-visitor-message-from-synapse#post1408635\" target=\"_blank\">Check my Exalted homebrew</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/32-synapse","name":"Synapse","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar32_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1507"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-17T22:24:38-07:00","id":1403342,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Lack of Victims </div> </div> </div>This is false to me.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/904596-geniuses-and-gramarie\" target=\"_blank\">Genius templates</a>: Super Science Mini-Template for Demon: the Descent<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1337874-oracle-the-endbringers-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Oracle the Endbringers</a>: Time-Manipulator Fan-Splat","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","name":"milo v3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8331_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1505"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-18T01:12:06-07:00","id":1403350,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>arthexis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1402680-lex-magica-and-time-travel?p=1403172#post1403172\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Morality makes sense only <i>f</i>or people that cannot rewind time.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> That sounds like an exceedingly Hubristic quote from an Arcanthus whom if they really meant it more than a pithy quip and actually sincerely followed that philosophy to its logical conclusion would end up without any Wisdom at all.<br /> <br /> In Subjective time you still experience all of your moral failings. Also paying the piper for this type moral of hubris (The &quot;All evil is permissible in the name of undoing said evil/mistake with time travel&quot; hubris) in time travel is practically a trope. Cite: <a href=\"https://www.imdb.com/title/tt2669336/\" target=\"_blank\">Time Lapse</a><br /> <br /> Could be seen as an Time Arcanum artifact running in a Sanctum to make photos, whose photos falling into the hands of mundane neighbors across the way.<br /> <br /> Similar type of hubristic logic used in movie <a href=\"https://www.imdb.com/title/tt0216216/\" target=\"_blank\">6th Day</a>, about killing people and replacing them with clones whose memory didn't include the evil being discovered.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>milo v3</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1402680-lex-magica-and-time-travel?p=1403342#post1403342\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is false to me.</div> </div> </div> </div>That was in response to &quot;lack of victims&quot;<br /> <br /> I'd like to point out if time rewinding is happening to &quot;undo&quot; unwise or evil actions there still are DEFINITELY victims, they just exist outside the primary timeline. Also it isn't like the alternate time is not viewable with proper Time magic. In the Mage universe it take Time 5 to actually erase something, those rewinds are just threads dangling from the main timeline but that stuff actually still happened and the shifting sands mage still remembers it. What horrific kind of abuser could and would likely exist if hubristic acts that could be remembered by the perpetrator in alternate timelines not &quot;count&quot; against wisdom for free? Inuring has a cost, and while those abusers could still exist in niche, there wouldn't be a whole Arcanum which by default and without extra effort bypassed a primary thematic enforcing mechanic of the gameline.<br /> <br /> In fact I would argue doing something that would be a breaking point or even considered only as morally bad as assault (of whatever type) with the intention of rewinding time to get the experience without the main timeline victim knowing, (so you <i>only</i> victimized the alternate timeline version) is at least as bad as using magic to control a being or entity against its will, which it could be argued is exactly an apt description.<br /> <br /> All of the above is about the morality and wisdom of rewinding time to undo acts of hubris or cruelty.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":3279,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","user":"Pale_Crusader","date":". 07-18-2020, 04:48 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}